1
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:11,140
Brighten up the room and try stepping away for a bit,
will you?

2
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:42,940
Things will work out somehow, eventually.

3
00:00:55,920 --> 00:00:59,880
Anyone without work, come to my place.

4
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:04,520
I don't have any either, but don't worry.

5
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:11,620
Look, even the steel is burning.

6
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:18,260
Things will work out somehow, eventually.

7
00:01:20,380 --> 00:01:23,540
You get it, right? I get it, I get it.

8
00:01:23,540 --> 00:01:27,420
If you understand, follow me and keep quiet.

9
00:01:47,020 --> 00:01:48,500
Just being coy

10
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:50,800
Ryo-san, have you eaten any mochi?

11
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:55,120
Mochi? Now that you mention it,
it's New Year's and I haven't had any mochi.

12
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:58,840
It's the leftover osechi,
but please eat this.

13
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:00,580
I'll grill the mochi now.

14
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:02,400
Osechi

15
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:06,760
Back in the day, New Year's used to
make you feel your spirit tighten up.

16
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:10,580
These days, it doesn't hit me at all.

17
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:12,760
Here you go, Ryo-san. Sorry for the wait.

18
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:16,240
Hey, this, this... it's starting to hit me!

19
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:25,840
Ryo-san, if you eat calmly,

20
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:30,020
Japanese elementary
schoolers still love mochi

21
00:02:30,020 --> 00:02:33,700
Well, watching you eat is satisfying,
Ryo-san.

22
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:35,060
I see.

23
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:35,200
Whoa!

24
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:42,220
How unusual. A kamishibai?

25
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:46,080
Yamato Spirit Preservation Society!

26
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:52,420
It's quite authentic.

27
00:02:52,660 --> 00:02:54,220
It's the Japanese-language big brother!

28
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:54,900
Lucky!

29
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:57,020
The Japanese-language big brother?

30
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:00,540
The Society for Protecting
the Japanese Spirit.

31
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:07,220
Let's use correct Japanese this year, too.

32
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:08,360
Okay!

33
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:13,440
And now, the story of how
White-Beard Grandpa came to visit!

34
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:15,220
Once upon a time...

35
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:17,800
White-Beard Grandpa?

36
00:03:18,940 --> 00:03:23,000
From the northern land came the holy,
legendary White-Beard Grandpa.

37
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:26,220
Ringing his jingle bells, he arrived.

38
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:31,020
Of course, he came to hand out
new goods to all the good children.

39
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:33,300
That's Santa Claus, isn't it?

40
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:36,960
Santa Claus.

41
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:39,000
Jingle

42
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,500
since that house had no chimney,

43
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:48,100
White-Beard Grandpa came in through the front door and greeted them with
"Kin ga Seitansai."

44
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:53,140
Could "Kin ga Seitansai
" possibly mean "Merry Christmas"?

45
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:55,920
That's too literal, I don't get it at all.

46
00:03:55,920 --> 00:04:01,820
White-Beard Grandpa went to the bedside of the
children sleeping soundly in their three-tier bunk beds

47
00:04:01,820 --> 00:04:05,160
and left new goods,
then returned to the northern land.

48
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:08,760
The next morning,
the children were overjoyed.

49
00:04:09,220 --> 00:04:12,740
The eldest son, Taro,
got the electronic camera he wanted,

50
00:04:12,740 --> 00:04:16,380
the eldest daughter,
Hanako, got the small disc

51
00:04:16,380 --> 00:04:19,700
and the second son, Jiro,
got a Western kemari ball.

52
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:24,400
Digital camera, Mo-Moms CD, soccer ball

53
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:26,520
So that's how it sounds
when you put it in Japanese.

54
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:28,280
That's an eye-opener.

55
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:31,120
Why do they force everything into Japanese?

56
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:34,040
It's about protecting
the Japanese spirit, right?

57
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:37,860
I bet they're trying to show the children
how wonderful the Japanese language is.

58
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:41,360
And they all lived happily ever after.

59
00:04:42,060 --> 00:04:45,320
Now, to all the good children who
watched the kamishibai to the end,

60
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:47,960
let me give you some sweet plum senbei.

61
00:04:50,180 --> 00:04:51,280
Thanks, big brother.

62
00:04:51,280 --> 00:04:55,020
I endured it and watched
the whole thing too!

63
00:04:56,420 --> 00:04:59,400
Anyway,
why are they doing a Christmas story now?

64
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:04,500
The handmade New Year's story
just didn't get finished in time.

65
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:07,820
Since it's New Year's,
today there's one more,

66
00:05:07,820 --> 00:05:10,380
and I'll give New Year's
money to all the good children.

67
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:14,060
Alright, take one of whatever you like.

68
00:05:16,460 --> 00:05:17,180
What's this?

69
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:21,440
That's called kite string.
You fly kites with it.

70
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:24,880
Try playing with traditional Japanese
toys like these once in a while.

71
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:27,680
Hey, this octopus has a weird shape.

72
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:28,400
Does it fly?

73
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:31,440
Yeah, it goes up really high!

74
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,280
Look, you just let out the string like this.

75
00:05:35,220 --> 00:05:36,720
Oh, it got tangled!

76
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:38,200
Well

77
00:05:38,740 --> 00:05:40,040
Hey, let me try.

78
00:05:40,520 --> 00:05:44,520
New Year's means kite flying,
and kite flying means New Year's, right?

79
00:05:44,520 --> 00:05:46,820
Have you kids never flown a kite before?

80
00:05:47,060 --> 00:05:47,960
Yes!

81
00:05:49,740 --> 00:05:52,440
There aren't many places
left to fly kites these days,

82
00:05:52,440 --> 00:05:54,740
and for someone like me to be so careless!

83
00:05:54,740 --> 00:05:57,420
I never realized the string
had gotten into such a mess.

84
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:00,160
Welcome, come on in.

85
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:02,040
Are you all ready?

86
00:06:02,040 --> 00:06:03,920
Anytime you're ready, young lady.

87
00:06:03,920 --> 00:06:04,840
Here we go.

88
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:08,380
Okay, let go of it.

89
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:16,920
Watch closely. This is how you fly a kite.

90
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:20,720
Flying a kite is fun, isn't it?

91
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:25,860
It's a good thing, now and then.

92
00:06:25,860 --> 00:06:29,540
We're Japanese,
so kite flying is for New Year's, right?

93
00:06:33,940 --> 00:06:36,960
You're so young, yet so admirable.
I'm deeply moved.

94
00:06:37,420 --> 00:06:38,320
I'm honored.

95
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:40,900
Through the reckless
overuse of foreign words,

96
00:06:40,900 --> 00:06:44,140
our Japanese language is
on the brink of collapse.

97
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:46,840
I want to protect beautiful Japanese,

98
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:51,680
and revive Japan's ancient culture
and customs that are being forgotten.

99
00:06:52,340 --> 00:06:55,940
If you like,
would you care to stay for some warm tea?

100
00:06:55,940 --> 00:06:57,800
Are you sure?

101
00:06:58,740 --> 00:07:01,940
It's just me and Terai here anyway.
Go ahead, it's fine.

102
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:11,500
Happy New Year! Here's a souvenir.

103
00:07:12,980 --> 00:07:14,160
This is

104
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:16,340
We met at the park over there.

105
00:07:16,340 --> 00:07:18,260
My name is Fujii Yamato.

106
00:07:18,260 --> 00:07:19,680
Nice to meet you.

107
00:07:21,680 --> 00:07:23,040
Wow, it's delicious.

108
00:07:23,040 --> 00:07:25,580
Japanese people really do prefer Japanese tea,
don't they?

109
00:07:25,900 --> 00:07:26,260
Yeah, exactly.

110
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:30,980
It's chocolate as a souvenir,
but I hope it suits your taste.

111
00:07:31,720 --> 00:07:32,223
Actually,

112
00:07:32,273 --> 00:07:40,020
I love that tropical evergreen nut-filled South American bean powder and milk paste confection,
so I'll gladly accept it.

113
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:41,240
I see.

114
00:07:42,260 --> 00:07:42,620
Amazing.

115
00:07:43,120 --> 00:07:44,637
Right? Yamato-kun is good at

116
00:07:44,687 --> 00:07:47,980
talking without using foreign words.

117
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:50,900
Terai's become a total Yamato fan, you know.

118
00:07:50,900 --> 00:07:54,000
If you're going to say that, please say
"passionate note-taking" instead.

119
00:07:54,820 --> 00:07:56,720
You can put everything into Japanese?

120
00:07:56,980 --> 00:07:57,940
Well, yeah, more or less.

121
00:07:58,560 --> 00:08:01,000
Then what do you call a plastic model?

122
00:08:01,460 --> 00:08:03,920
A synthetic-resin assembly model, I suppose.

123
00:08:04,380 --> 00:08:05,280
And TV?

124
00:08:06,020 --> 00:08:07,940
It's a box-type original-sharp
receiving machine.

125
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:11,840
And the electric guitar?

126
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:14,720
That's the Western six-string
electric jamisen, right?

127
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:15,940
Give it back!

128
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:18,860
What about canned coffee?

129
00:08:19,040 --> 00:08:21,680
It's canned South American bean broth.

130
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:23,280
What are you saying!

131
00:08:23,400 --> 00:08:23,780
Did you get it!

132
00:08:24,320 --> 00:08:26,320
H-hey, what do you think you're doing!

133
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:28,600
What about the lighter?

134
00:08:28,700 --> 00:08:30,860
A portable manual ignition device.

135
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:35,220
And this head?

136
00:08:36,900 --> 00:08:37,780
Well...

137
00:08:39,300 --> 00:08:39,840
Sea urchin.

138
00:08:41,080 --> 00:08:43,980
Wow! You can express everything in Japanese!

139
00:08:44,160 --> 00:08:46,180
That's right! He really is amazing!

140
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:51,100
Well then, sea urchin.

141
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:52,260
No way, you're lying.

142
00:08:52,260 --> 00:08:53,140
What?

143
00:08:53,140 --> 00:08:54,440
It's nothing.

144
00:08:55,280 --> 00:08:59,020
I think we've been using English
far too casually until now.

145
00:08:59,020 --> 00:09:02,420
This might be a good chance to
reexamine the Japanese language.

146
00:09:02,420 --> 00:09:05,980
Hearing you say that makes coming
all the way here worthwhile.

147
00:09:05,980 --> 00:09:10,380
Splendid. You're a rare breed
of Japanese man these days.

148
00:09:10,380 --> 00:09:11,780
Not really.

149
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:16,080
It's cold.

150
00:09:17,980 --> 00:09:20,380
Is there still any of that
souvenir chocolate left?

151
00:09:20,540 --> 00:09:23,780
What are you talking about?
You ate it all three days ago, didn't you?

152
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:26,620
Damn, so that was the last of it.

153
00:09:27,300 --> 00:09:29,704
I was planning to save on food until payday,

154
00:09:29,754 --> 00:09:32,080
I should've divided it
into smaller portions.

155
00:09:32,680 --> 00:09:33,820
Honestly, I'm appalled.

156
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:37,905
Senpai. Earlier, that guy who
came by the other day, you know,

157
00:09:37,955 --> 00:09:41,840
the one obsessed with Japanese,
came looking for you.

158
00:09:41,980 --> 00:09:44,880
Yamato? I wonder what he wants?

159
00:09:45,420 --> 00:09:47,840
Is 6-chome around here?

160
00:09:47,840 --> 00:09:50,040
It's very much a shitamachi, isn't it?

161
00:09:50,040 --> 00:09:52,260
That must be Yamato's obsession, I suppose.

162
00:09:52,260 --> 00:09:54,380
Here it is, this is the house.

163
00:09:56,040 --> 00:09:56,700
Please.

164
00:09:57,400 --> 00:09:59,960
Senpai, that's so old-fashioned.

165
00:09:59,960 --> 00:10:01,980
Doesn't it look so Japanese?

166
00:10:01,980 --> 00:10:02,420
Yes.

167
00:10:03,140 --> 00:10:04,660
Welcome.

168
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:11,560
This is Yamato's place, you know.

169
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:14,100
It's your turn, Nakagawa.

170
00:10:14,760 --> 00:10:18,487
Don't worry.
I'm American, but I speak fluent Japanese,

171
00:10:18,537 --> 00:10:20,200
so you're a friend of Yamato's, aren't you?

172
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:23,620
Hello. Is she your wife, by any chance?

173
00:10:23,780 --> 00:10:24,100
Yes.

174
00:10:25,540 --> 00:10:26,600
I'm Catherine.

175
00:10:33,820 --> 00:10:35,108
Well, I'm surprised.

176
00:10:35,158 --> 00:10:38,840
Yamato's missus, the one who
hates foreigners, is an American?

177
00:10:39,420 --> 00:10:42,980
So I don't actually hate foreign countries,
you see.

178
00:10:44,880 --> 00:10:48,400
I'm American,
but in my heart I'm totally Japanese.

179
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:52,380
Your Japanese is very good.
When did you come to Japan?

180
00:10:52,380 --> 00:10:56,280
I first came to Japan during college,
on my first trip abroad.

181
00:10:56,880 --> 00:11:00,220
I moved here a year ago,
when I married Yamato.

182
00:11:00,700 --> 00:11:04,680
I've loved Japan since I was
a child and studied it a lot.

183
00:11:05,300 --> 00:11:08,120
Your impression of Japan must
have been quite different, right?

184
00:11:08,120 --> 00:11:08,780
Yes.

185
00:11:10,220 --> 00:11:10,580
Like this.

186
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:12,660
What era is this?

187
00:11:14,100 --> 00:11:14,880
And then...

188
00:11:15,440 --> 00:11:16,820
Hey, is this a public bath?

189
00:11:20,140 --> 00:11:22,480
But then I met Yamato at just that time,

190
00:11:22,480 --> 00:11:25,720
and he was exactly the
Japanese man I had imagined.

191
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:28,420
Earnest and sincere,

192
00:11:28,420 --> 00:11:32,200
he was the very image of a true Japanese man,
and I fell for him at first sight.

193
00:11:33,440 --> 00:11:34,720
How wonderful.

194
00:11:36,260 --> 00:11:38,840
Something so unpleasant right
at the start of the new year.

195
00:11:39,440 --> 00:11:41,280
When it comes to the new year,
there's New Year's.

196
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:45,126
When it comes to April, there's kite flying,

197
00:11:45,176 --> 00:11:48,380
battledore and spinning tops. How elegant.

198
00:11:48,940 --> 00:11:50,258
A Japan like this, nowadays...

199
00:11:50,308 --> 00:11:55,100
That's only sold at the Asakusa branch or the Tokyo Tower gift shop,
you know.

200
00:11:56,480 --> 00:11:57,480
Really, it is.

201
00:11:58,000 --> 00:11:58,979
That's why we,

202
00:11:59,029 --> 00:12:02,560
want to pass on even a little of
Japan's wonders to the children.

203
00:12:03,060 --> 00:12:06,460
Yamato teaches the kids old-fashioned games

204
00:12:06,460 --> 00:12:10,040
between his work, and I teach the
children abacus and calligraphy.

205
00:12:10,440 --> 00:12:12,580
She's the one who wrote the
characters on the kite, too.

206
00:12:13,020 --> 00:12:14,360
Her handwriting is better than mine,
you know.

207
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:16,900
Your handwriting is
impossible to read, senpai.

208
00:12:18,160 --> 00:12:21,800
I'm looking forward to the day
I get to fly a kite with Yamato.

209
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:24,780
Well, we flew kites the other
day during the picture-story show,

210
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:31,740
That day there was no wind and the humidity was low,
and the crows weren't cawing,

211
00:12:31,740 --> 00:12:34,100
so Yamato said it wasn't
good kite-flying weather.

212
00:12:36,100 --> 00:12:38,360
Let's fly a kite together next year,
for sure.

213
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:38,980
Yeah.

214
00:12:40,040 --> 00:12:42,060
Yamato is really good at flying kites,
you know.

215
00:12:42,480 --> 00:12:43,360
Hey, Yamato.

216
00:12:43,800 --> 00:12:44,160
Yeah?

217
00:12:46,880 --> 00:12:47,554
By the way,

218
00:12:47,604 --> 00:12:51,660
did you invite us just to
show off your beautiful wife?

219
00:12:51,860 --> 00:12:52,220
Yeah.

220
00:12:57,240 --> 00:12:58,040
Please close the door.

221
00:12:58,040 --> 00:12:59,380
Uh, y-yes.

222
00:13:01,300 --> 00:13:01,660
What!

223
00:13:04,100 --> 00:13:05,100
Come on in.

224
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:07,140
Is this a ninja house or something?

225
00:13:07,620 --> 00:13:08,320
This way.

226
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:29,240
What the heck is this?

227
00:13:29,620 --> 00:13:31,260
Oh my Gods!

228
00:13:31,260 --> 00:13:32,220
Yamato-kun

229
00:13:32,220 --> 00:13:33,080
Not a fan.

230
00:13:33,080 --> 00:13:35,180
No matter how much I search, it's not here.

231
00:13:35,180 --> 00:13:38,480
The Michael Jordan-signed shoes I just got

232
00:13:38,480 --> 00:13:39,680
Calm down.

233
00:13:40,040 --> 00:13:41,840
Tell me, what's going on with you!

234
00:13:42,160 --> 00:13:43,320
Yeah, that's right!

235
00:13:43,740 --> 00:13:46,660
Ever since I was a kid,
I've loved America sooo much,

236
00:13:46,660 --> 00:13:48,100
I'm just an ordinary Japanese guy!

237
00:13:48,900 --> 00:13:50,940
Basketball, football,

238
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:52,760
American comics, Hollywood,

239
00:13:52,780 --> 00:13:54,100
rock and roll!

240
00:13:54,440 --> 00:13:55,880
America only!

241
00:13:55,940 --> 00:13:57,040
Viva America!

242
00:13:57,400 --> 00:13:59,400
I'm a damn America-worshipping fool!

243
00:14:00,020 --> 00:14:02,800
How could a guy like that ever
protect the Japanese spirit?

244
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:03,920
I'm doing something, you know!

245
00:14:04,680 --> 00:14:06,920
Yeah, it just kind of happened,
you could say.

246
00:14:06,920 --> 00:14:09,580
Catherine loves Japan,
just like she always does,

247
00:14:09,580 --> 00:14:12,460
and she's convinced I'm the same,

248
00:14:13,400 --> 00:14:15,080
so it couldn't be helped that
things turned out this way.

249
00:14:15,460 --> 00:14:15,940
Check!

250
00:14:15,940 --> 00:14:18,000
Welcome!

251
00:14:18,640 --> 00:14:19,620
Three years ago, in the summer,

252
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:22,820
I was saving up money for a trip to America

253
00:14:22,820 --> 00:14:24,800
by working part-time at an izakaya.

254
00:14:25,320 --> 00:14:28,300
Then, by chance,
Catherine happened to walk by.

255
00:14:33,620 --> 00:14:34,880
It was love at first sight.

256
00:14:36,280 --> 00:14:39,440
Catherine thinks Mīro is the
very model of a Japanese man.

257
00:14:39,440 --> 00:14:40,620
She believes it.

258
00:14:40,620 --> 00:14:41,480
Why?

259
00:14:41,700 --> 00:14:43,700
No! It's completely different!

260
00:14:44,180 --> 00:14:45,760
Because of that, his trip to America

261
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:46,440
Bah!

262
00:14:47,360 --> 00:14:49,440
is now managing an izakaya.

263
00:14:50,140 --> 00:14:52,140
Chu-hi! Umezawa's meat and potato stew.

264
00:14:52,140 --> 00:14:53,020
You know!

265
00:14:53,640 --> 00:14:56,020
Trying to win over Catherine,
the Japanese beauty,

266
00:14:56,020 --> 00:14:58,860
he kept up the act of an
old-fashioned Japanese man,

267
00:14:58,860 --> 00:15:00,480
and couldn't back down anymore,

268
00:15:01,080 --> 00:15:02,900
so he hid his true self.

269
00:15:02,900 --> 00:15:05,400
He ended up living a double life, didn't he?

270
00:15:07,980 --> 00:15:10,080
If it gets out,
she'll definitely divorce me.

271
00:15:10,080 --> 00:15:10,960
No way.

272
00:15:11,420 --> 00:15:14,080
Are you really planning to
keep living like this forever?

273
00:15:14,880 --> 00:15:16,980
This room is my America.

274
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:22,160
Once I step out of here, for Catherine's sake,
I'll become Yamato, a true Japanese man.

275
00:15:22,960 --> 00:15:24,080
I'll pull it off.

276
00:15:30,040 --> 00:15:30,680
That...

277
00:15:32,860 --> 00:15:35,700
This must be where Akis got in.

278
00:15:36,280 --> 00:15:39,320
Of all things,
the rarest Michael Jordan shoes.

279
00:15:39,320 --> 00:15:41,780
Shit! Unbelievable!

280
00:15:41,960 --> 00:15:43,260
Come back, Michael!

281
00:15:43,820 --> 00:15:45,460
Ryo-san, please.

282
00:15:45,460 --> 00:15:47,620
Please get my treasure back.

283
00:15:47,620 --> 00:15:50,740
Okay, okay, I get it. Just calm down.

284
00:15:55,080 --> 00:15:57,220
What were you two talking about, Yamato?

285
00:15:58,380 --> 00:16:00,360
Just man talk.

286
00:16:07,280 --> 00:16:07,320
Mm.

287
00:16:12,840 --> 00:16:13,240
Mm.

288
00:16:16,080 --> 00:16:17,760
Akis got caught!

289
00:16:20,760 --> 00:16:22,440
Chief!

290
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:25,929
That's the Akis who's
been terrorizing this area.

291
00:16:25,979 --> 00:16:28,900
He was proudly wearing them around his neck,
bold as anything.

292
00:16:29,320 --> 00:16:31,360
Here you go. This is it, right?

293
00:16:31,860 --> 00:16:33,620
Thank you so much. Thank goodness.

294
00:16:34,040 --> 00:16:35,520
Yamato, you're back?

295
00:16:36,760 --> 00:16:39,700
I forgot something,
so I'm heading back to the shop right away.

296
00:16:41,380 --> 00:16:42,784
Are you all right, Yamato?

297
00:16:42,834 --> 00:16:46,874
How long do you plan to keep this up?
You'll be found out sooner or later.

298
00:16:46,924 --> 00:16:49,860
Even the gods who believe in
you can't stand by and watch.

299
00:16:51,180 --> 00:16:52,600
I'll tell them someday.

300
00:16:52,600 --> 00:16:54,720
I see. Well, see you.

301
00:16:56,900 --> 00:16:57,400
What is it?

302
00:16:58,240 --> 00:17:00,660
Could you teach me how
to fly a kite next time?

303
00:17:00,660 --> 00:17:03,240
I've never flown one before.

304
00:17:03,240 --> 00:17:05,720
It's no trouble at all. See you.

305
00:17:07,500 --> 00:17:08,000
Thank you very much.

306
00:17:15,480 --> 00:17:16,640
Yamato?

307
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:25,940
What is this? Is this Yamato's room?
Wow, this is...

308
00:17:27,340 --> 00:17:31,500
Yamato liked basketball, right?

309
00:17:33,260 --> 00:17:41,720
He liked Michael Jordan and Clint Eastwood?
Catherine, was it a lie that he said he liked Chiyonofuji and Ken Takakura?

310
00:17:42,140 --> 00:17:46,020
And was it a lie too that he said
natto and pickles were all he needed?

311
00:17:46,380 --> 00:17:46,960
That's right.

312
00:17:47,400 --> 00:17:49,460
I'm not some old-fashioned
Japanese guy at all.

313
00:17:49,460 --> 00:17:52,480
Truth is, I like hamburgers more than rice.

314
00:17:58,840 --> 00:18:00,980
I, Yamato, am hiding something.

315
00:18:00,980 --> 00:18:03,920
I kept wondering,
what could it be, what could it be...

316
00:18:04,360 --> 00:18:06,020
Why didn't you tell me?

317
00:18:07,080 --> 00:18:08,020
If I'd told you the truth...

318
00:18:08,420 --> 00:18:11,020
Wouldn't you have just brushed me off?

319
00:18:11,720 --> 00:18:15,180
What about you, Yamato?
Did you marry me just because I'm American?

320
00:18:15,560 --> 00:18:18,380
Was I just another part
of your collection here?

321
00:18:18,700 --> 00:18:19,560
N-no, that's not it!

322
00:18:20,440 --> 00:18:23,020
I'm not that narrow-minded a woman!

323
00:18:23,340 --> 00:18:25,600
It's just that I'm sad Yamato lied to me!

324
00:18:26,560 --> 00:18:27,120
Idiot!

325
00:18:31,640 --> 00:18:32,360
Catherine!

326
00:18:34,800 --> 00:18:35,860
Catherine!

327
00:18:40,780 --> 00:18:41,640
Catherine!

328
00:18:44,460 --> 00:18:47,180
Catherine's gone.

329
00:18:48,220 --> 00:18:49,160
Did she find out?

330
00:18:49,160 --> 00:18:49,640
Yes.

331
00:18:51,020 --> 00:18:52,980
We've searched everywhere around here.

332
00:18:53,580 --> 00:18:54,900
But she's nowhere to be found.

333
00:18:55,580 --> 00:18:57,360
Please, you have to help me!

334
00:18:57,900 --> 00:19:00,180
I can't live without Catherine!

335
00:19:00,180 --> 00:19:02,720
All right, all right,
pull yourself together.

336
00:19:02,720 --> 00:19:05,500
She didn't go back to America, did she?

337
00:19:05,900 --> 00:19:18,480
Uuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuuu...

338
00:19:19,520 --> 00:19:20,760
It's all my fault. I was such an idiot.

339
00:19:21,960 --> 00:19:24,980
In the end,
I never got to fly a kite with Catherine.

340
00:19:26,080 --> 00:19:27,520
That's right, the kite!

341
00:19:29,280 --> 00:19:37,380
Yamato

342
00:19:37,860 --> 00:19:39,300
No, please stop.

343
00:19:39,300 --> 00:19:43,060
Don't say that. Come have fun with us, miss.

344
00:19:44,700 --> 00:19:47,240
Come on. Just one drink with us.

345
00:19:47,840 --> 00:19:48,580
Sir.

346
00:19:53,520 --> 00:19:55,240
What is it, buddy?

347
00:19:56,820 --> 00:19:58,260
Another drink?

348
00:19:58,260 --> 00:19:59,940
Why don't you have a drink at our place?

349
00:19:59,940 --> 00:20:03,580
The food is great,
and we have every kind of drink.

350
00:20:03,580 --> 00:20:05,600
Any kind of liquor.

351
00:20:05,600 --> 00:20:09,540
Yes, we even have the legendary sake Otokozakari,
and Kippon.

352
00:20:09,540 --> 00:20:11,020
Come on, come on in.

353
00:20:11,020 --> 00:20:12,640
Otokozakari Nice.

354
00:20:14,800 --> 00:20:15,320
Thanks.

355
00:20:15,320 --> 00:20:15,820
Thank you.

356
00:20:34,080 --> 00:20:35,420
It's been a while.

357
00:20:38,360 --> 00:20:39,220
Catherine!

358
00:20:42,300 --> 00:20:42,980
You're late.

359
00:20:42,980 --> 00:20:46,140
Sorry, sorry.
I couldn't get away from work easily.

360
00:20:47,700 --> 00:20:48,280
Are you angry?

361
00:20:49,820 --> 00:20:50,380
No.

362
00:20:58,300 --> 00:20:59,160
This way.

363
00:21:03,980 --> 00:21:06,060
Here, here. I forgot about this spot.

364
00:21:07,480 --> 00:21:08,700
Alright, then let's go.

365
00:21:11,300 --> 00:21:13,560
Alright, a good breeze is picking up.

366
00:21:13,560 --> 00:21:14,900
Let's go all out.

367
00:21:17,460 --> 00:21:20,600
Alright, Junpei, Kappei, you two go too.

368
00:21:20,600 --> 00:21:21,140
Yeah.

369
00:21:21,140 --> 00:21:24,281
Don't panic,

370
00:21:24,331 --> 00:21:28,800
pull the string hard.

371
00:21:28,800 --> 00:21:29,380
Good.

372
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:31,080
Next, I'm going.

373
00:21:31,080 --> 00:21:31,840
Here I go.

374
00:21:31,840 --> 00:21:33,020
Okay.

375
00:21:33,020 --> 00:21:36,060
Wow, that's amazing.

376
00:21:36,780 --> 00:21:38,520
You can't just be happy about it yet.

377
00:21:39,700 --> 00:21:42,900
Your turn next!
Give it everything you've got!

378
00:21:43,140 --> 00:21:43,940
Y-yes!

379
00:21:44,480 --> 00:21:46,080
Good luck! Yamato-kun!

380
00:21:46,460 --> 00:21:46,820
Alright!

381
00:21:54,960 --> 00:21:55,840
Yah!

382
00:21:59,340 --> 00:22:00,740
Ready? Let go!

383
00:22:01,100 --> 00:22:01,460
Yes!

384
00:22:03,860 --> 00:22:04,820
Pull the string!

385
00:22:09,700 --> 00:22:12,540
Don't panic! Just keep reeling
the string in toward the kite!

386
00:22:12,980 --> 00:22:15,480
With the wind! Get the kite riding the wind!

387
00:22:19,220 --> 00:22:21,160
We did it! It's rising, it's rising!

388
00:22:22,520 --> 00:22:24,160
Good! This is great!

389
00:22:30,540 --> 00:22:31,800
Line up, everyone!

390
00:22:36,220 --> 00:22:38,800
Yamato,
you're at the far end on the other side!

391
00:22:39,700 --> 00:22:41,640
Right now, you're between Junpei and Kappei!

392
00:22:42,240 --> 00:22:43,380
Don't let the strings get tangled!

393
00:22:50,620 --> 00:22:52,980
Yamato, God is watching you!

394
00:22:58,860 --> 00:22:59,800
Catherine...

395
00:23:00,540 --> 00:23:02,860
Catherine! This is how I feel!

396
00:23:16,040 --> 00:23:18,820
I love you.

397
00:23:22,240 --> 00:23:23,000
Me too.

398
00:23:30,920 --> 00:23:34,180
Breakfast will still be rice and miso soup,
like always.

399
00:23:34,180 --> 00:23:35,940
I won't give up on this one thing.

400
00:23:37,420 --> 00:23:39,260
Bread once every three days,

401
00:23:39,740 --> 00:23:41,040
What are you talking about?

402
00:23:41,040 --> 00:23:43,260
Tofu and spinach ohitashi, you know.

403
00:23:45,160 --> 00:23:46,400
and crispy bacon.

404
00:23:48,400 --> 00:23:50,500
Then, once a week for Shu, okay?

405
00:23:51,680 --> 00:23:52,580
Yay!

406
00:24:17,760 --> 00:24:26,520
Thank you for watching.

407
00:24:26,580 --> 00:24:31,460
Even in sandals, he can run so fast.
I wonder why.

408
00:24:31,460 --> 00:24:33,140
I wonder why.

409
00:24:33,980 --> 00:24:39,420
Why does Ryo-san always have his sleeves rolled up?
I wonder why.

410
00:24:39,420 --> 00:24:40,560
I wonder why.

411
00:24:40,980 --> 00:24:45,720
He's a complete mess, yet everyone looks up to him.
I wonder why.

412
00:24:45,720 --> 00:24:47,840
I wonder why.

413
00:24:48,660 --> 00:24:51,640
It doesn't really matter, but

414
00:24:52,900 --> 00:24:58,300
why is this anime's title so long?

415
00:24:59,080 --> 00:25:01,160
This is Kameari Park Police Box,
Katsushika Ward.

416
00:25:01,160 --> 00:25:02,840
I wonder why.

417
00:25:03,620 --> 00:25:05,340
I wonder why.

418
00:25:07,140 --> 00:25:08,520
I wonder why.

419
00:25:08,520 --> 00:25:09,720
I wonder why.

420
00:25:10,200 --> 00:25:11,100
I wonder why.

421
00:25:12,720 --> 00:25:14,120
I wonder why.

422
00:25:14,120 --> 00:25:15,460
I wonder why.

423
00:25:15,460 --> 00:25:16,800
I wonder why.

424
00:25:16,800 --> 00:25:16,940
Why?

425
00:25:16,940 --> 00:25:17,380
Why?

426
00:25:17,380 --> 00:25:18,080
Why?

427
00:25:18,080 --> 00:25:18,980
I wonder why.

428
00:25:30,340 --> 00:25:32,420
There he goes again,
that crackpot professor!

429
00:25:32,420 --> 00:25:34,340
What? A perverted mask?

430
00:25:34,800 --> 00:25:37,460
Are you saying that if you put this on,
you can become any face?

431
00:25:37,760 --> 00:25:40,040
This is just like the Kaijin Nijūmensō,
isn't it?

432
00:25:41,120 --> 00:25:43,400
Alright, let's pull a prank with this!

433
00:25:43,780 --> 00:25:47,120
Next time, Ryo-san,
you're up as the Nijūmensō, okay?

434
00:25:48,980 --> 00:25:50,900
Over there is Kamearii.

435
00:25:52,040 --> 00:25:54,360
Well, I bet it's Kamearii.

436
00:25:55,460 --> 00:25:59,240
Everyone, come visit our town!

437
00:26:00,380 --> 00:26:00,860
Thank you.

